\section{Experiments}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types (63\%) lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). This scope limits generalizability: conclusions apply to the included subset, not to all ScienceWorld tasks. An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms that death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types, with a mean $\Delta\text{DR} = +38.2$\,pp exceeding the included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}).

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task scatter: death rate differential (internal $-$ deterministic) vs.\ performance gap ($\Delta_{\text{full}}$). $r = 0.97$ (Spearman's $\rho = 0.986$; Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}), $p < 10^{-6}$, $n = 11$ task types. Since $\Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ with $P$ constant, $r \to 1$ as DAF $\to 1$; the near-perfect correlation is a structural consequence of death dominance, not an independent finding (\S\ref{sec:death-decomp}).}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

Of the 95 non-tie routing labels, 75 (78.9\%) arise from death asymmetry: the internal strategy triggers a catastrophic $-100$ penalty while the deterministic strategy survives. This penalty-independent label fraction is the primary diagnostic metric. Of these 75, 54 are sole-signal labels (both strategies score zero without death, so death avoidance was the \emph{sole} discriminating signal). At $P{=}0$, the 54 sole-signal labels reduce to ties, leaving 41 non-tie labels of which 21 (51.2\%) still involve death asymmetry; the labeling function itself is penalty-independent (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}), but the practical routing value of death avoidance requires $P > 0$.

Of 245 episodes, 150 (61.2\%) are ties---neither strategy outperforms the other---indicating that routing is relevant in only 38.8\% of episodes. Among these, death avoidance dominates. The Death Avoidance Factor, a derived penalty-dependent measure, quantifies death's contribution to the total performance gap: DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$ ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$; 95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$, episode-level bootstrap, $B{=}10{,}000$). DAF varies with penalty magnitude ($50.4\%$ at $P{=}10$; $82.6\%$ at $P{=}50$; Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}), confirming that the 78.9\% label fraction provides the more robust characterization. When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} \to 0$, DAF $\to 1$ is algebraic necessity regardless of actual death dominance; conversely, when $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (as in Reflexion $N{\geq}3$; \S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}), DAF exceeds 100\%, reflecting that death asymmetry more than accounts for the full gap.

\paragraph{Per-task correlation structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic (2.6$\times$), consistent with the internal strategy's reliance on self-generated observations that compound prediction errors across steps. Per-task, death rate differential correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$; Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}). Since $P$ is constant across all task types, $\Delta_{\text{full}} = P \cdot \delta + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$ makes $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ a linear function of $\delta$ with residual $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$; when DAF $\rightarrow 1$, this residual vanishes and $r \rightarrow 1$ by construction. The near-perfect correlation is therefore a mathematical consequence of death dominance, not an independent empirical finding. We retain it as an internal consistency check. The correlation drops below significance at $P \leq 25$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Of 245 episodes, 86 are \texttt{det\_preferred}, 9 \texttt{internal\_preferred}, and 150 \texttt{no\_preference}.

\paragraph{Signal concentration and binary structure.}
The Signal Concentration Index (SCI $= 0.2147$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}) confirms low concentration: the top-3 task types account for only $39.8\%$ of total death-rate differential. The aggregate 78.9\% reflects a per-task binary structure: all 10 death-prone task types produce 100\% death-asymmetry labels, while \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (zero deaths, $\Delta = +9.58$ from non-death scores) produces 0\%. The framework thus diagnoses a binary property (``is this environment death-dominated?'') rather than a continuous routing signal. Removing \texttt{find-non-living-thing} raises the label fraction to 100\% and DAF to 96.8\% (Appendix~\ref{app:loto-daf}).

\paragraph{Sensitivity and seed robustness.}
Non-clustered episode-level bootstrap ($B = 10{,}000$) yields 95\% CI $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for the death-avoidance label fraction, independent of cluster assumptions, with Cohen's $d = 3.02$ (interpret cautiously given $K{=}11$ task types). Leave-one-task-out analysis confirms stability (label fraction range: 75.9\%--78.9\%; Appendix~\ref{app:loto-daf}); an independent seed replication ($N{=}220$) yields DAF $= 89.7\%$, within 0.7~pp (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). As a conservative check, wild cluster bootstrap treating each task type as a cluster yields $p = 0.002$; however, with $K{=}11$ clusters this test falls below the $K{\geq}20$--$40$ threshold recommended for reliable inference~\citep{cameron-2008,webb-2023}, so we rely on the non-clustered bootstrap as primary. All 28 death-prone tasks share the same direction (Appendices~\ref{app:wild-cluster}--\ref{app:per-task-ci}).

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} cross-model replication across all 11 task types ($N{=}220$) confirms that death dominance replicates across model families: internal death rate $41.4\%$ vs.\ deterministic $11.4\%$ (death-rate differential $+30.0$~pp), DAF $= 88.2\%$ ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +33.17$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +3.9$), with direction consistency 10/10 on death-prone tasks, 83 deterministic-preferred, 3 internal-preferred (1.4\%, all death-asymmetry reversals), and 134 no-preference labels.
Both models exhibit nearly identical internal vulnerability (${\sim}$42\%: Qwen $42.9\%$, Llama $41.4\%$), confirming that death-prone failure is structurally determined; the differential magnitude varies ($+26.5$~pp Qwen, $+30.0$~pp Llama) because deterministic strategy safety is model-dependent ($16.3\%$ vs.\ $11.4\%$).
\texttt{identify-life-stages-1} shows cross-model precision matching ($55\%$/$10\%$/$+45$~pp in both models).
\texttt{find-non-living-thing} shows zero deaths in both models, consistent with its majority-class answer distribution (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Both models are in the 7--8B range; $10{\times}$ scaling (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) confirms that death dominance persists across capability levels (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$) & 30.6 & 255 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{10 death-prone tasks only:}} \\
Death-prone baseline & 48.0 & 200 \\
Retry ($5{\times}$) & 43.5 & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. Top: all 11 tasks (72B: independently collected at both internal and deterministic). Bottom: 10 death-prone tasks (\texttt{find-non-living-thing} excluded, 0\% death at all scales). All interventions reduce death rate but none approaches deterministic-level safety.}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry ($N{=}5$ attempts without self-correction) yields marginal reduction on death-prone tasks ($43.5\%$ vs.\ $48.0\%$ baseline, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp). Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} (verbal self-reflection between trials) reduces death to 31.4\% across all 11 tasks by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths, but the gap to deterministic remains 15.1~pp; on the 10 death-prone tasks, Reflexion achieves ${\sim}$35\%. Under Reflexion ($N{=}3$, matched-episode analysis), $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = -0.47$, yielding DAF $> 100\%$: the deterministic advantage is entirely attributable to death avoidance, and in non-death episodes the self-correcting agent slightly outperforms the deterministic baseline. Scaling to Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ parameters; both internal and deterministic strategies independently collected at 72B, 255 episodes across all 11 task types) reduces death rate to $30.6\%$. The 72B deterministic strategy achieves $2.75\%$ death rate ($\Delta\text{DR} = +27.8$~pp), and $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.28$, yielding DAF $= 92.4\%$ (7B: $90.4\%$); scaling lowers death rate but leaves death dominance intact.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We extend both interventions to $N{=}5$ trials on death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat ($48.0\% \to 43.5\%$, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp over five trials). Reflexion drops steeply to $34.0\%$ at $N{=}3$ ($\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $z = 2.75$, $p = 0.003$) then plateaus ($34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$, pairwise $p > 0.5$). With $n{=}200$ paired episodes, the minimum detectable effect is ${\sim}8$~pp ($\alpha = 0.05$, 80\% power): the key $N{=}1{\to}3$ decline is well-powered; the plateau claim ($N{=}3{\to}5$, $+1.0$~pp) is also supported, since a ${\geq}8$~pp improvement would be detected. We characterize this as suggestive evidence of a Reflexion ceiling rather than a conclusive demonstration. Retry's per-trial conditional death rate stays constant at ${\sim}48\%$; Reflexion's declines to ${\sim}31\%$ by Trial~3 (per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:dose-response}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Death rate as a function of trial count ($N$) for Retry and Reflexion on 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$). Retry (blue) is nearly flat; Reflexion (red) drops steeply through $N{=}3$ then plateaus. Horizontal lines: 72B scaling baseline ($30.6\%$, all 11 tasks) and deterministic baseline ($16.3\%$). Error bands: Wilson 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:dose-response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Environment Boundary Probes}
\label{sec:math-contrast}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We examine MATH and ALFWorld as boundary probes---not cross-domain validation---testing framework applicability across environments that instantiate ``deterministic computation'' differently (oracle simulator, code interpreter, admissible-action enumeration).

\paragraph{MATH.} On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$, code interpreter), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy ($73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$, McNemar $p = 0.81$), with 74.6\% no-preference. Without catastrophic failure, no routing signal emerges.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} (6 task types, 135 games) provides the deterministic strategy with admissible actions---an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch. Of 37 non-tie labels, 35 (94.6\%) favor the information-advantaged strategy, a one-directional dominance driven by information asymmetry rather than catastrophic failure. The framework diagnoses this qualitatively different mechanism without modification.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 20--40 episodes per type, 245 total). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---an \emph{oracle degeneracy} where an always-deterministic lookup achieves 90.5\% of non-tie oracle decisions (balanced accuracy 50.0\%: perfect deterministic recall, zero internal recall).

\subsection{Router Training: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside. The near-chance step-level accuracy admits two interpretations: (A)~episode-level labels projected onto steps create ${\sim}$88\% uninformative signal (60.3\% pre-event neutral, 27.4\% post-event), drowning learnable patterns in noise; (B)~routing signal is genuinely task-type-level---there is no step-level pattern to learn. Directional conflict is only 0.3\% of 2{,}040 training steps (labels are directionally correct in 99.7\% of informative steps), but the granularity mismatch leaves minimal signal for the router. The data scaling ablation (25\%--800\%, no improvement) and the extreme class imbalance of death-asymmetry episodes (86/95 $= 90.5\%$ deterministic-preferred) together favor Interpretation~B: routing signal is genuinely task-type-level, not step-level learnable. Definitive attribution between label noise and fundamental non-learnability remains an open question.

An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at chance. Episode-level independent evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below the majority-vote baseline of 50.0\%. Training uses episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode leakage. The high uninformative rate connects to noisy-label learning~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: standard ERM on labels with ${\sim}$88\% noise yields near-chance classifiers, consistent with our results.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

\section{Conclusion}

We introduced an episode-level counterfactual diagnostic framework for decomposing routing signal in modular LLM agents, overcoming per-step oracle degeneracy (97.4\% tie rate) through paired pure-strategy comparison with penalty-independent (label fraction) and penalty-dependent (DAF) metrics. Applied to ScienceWorld, 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels stem from death asymmetry---robust across four intervention axes, cross-model replication (Llama-3.1-8B, DAF${=}88.2\%$), and boundary probes (MATH, ALFWorld). The diagnostic value is practical: our 200 GPU-hour router training could not outperform the always-deterministic baseline, an outcome a 50-episode pilot would have predicted.

The framework applies to any environment with episode-level evaluation and binary catastrophic outcomes. Future work should target intermediate death regimes (NetHack combat tasks), develop methods to disentangle label noise from fundamental non-learnability in step-level routing, and extend to mixed-strategy routing and stronger backbones.

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Tool Use and External Computation in LLMs.}
A growing body of work augments language models with external tools \citep{tool-learning-survey-2024}, spanning several functional categories.
\emph{Self-supervised} approaches learn tool invocation from loss signals: Toolformer \cite{toolformer-2023} inserts API calls filtered by perplexity reduction; ToolACE \cite{toolace-2025} curates synthetic data for function-calling accuracy.
\emph{Token-level} methods embed tools into the model vocabulary: ToolkenGPT \cite{toolkengpt-2023} represents tools as special tokens resolved at inference.
\emph{Task-level} decomposition routes subtasks to specialized modules: ART \cite{art-2023} generates tool-augmented programs; Chameleon \cite{chameleon-2023} composes heterogeneous modules per subtask.
\emph{RL-based} methods learn routing policies from reward: ReTool \cite{retool-2025} discovers when code execution is preferable to chain-of-thought.
All focus on \emph{learning to use tools}; we ask a prior question: \emph{what signal drives the routing decision?}
Concurrent work examines \emph{when} to invoke tools: \citet{tool-overuse-illusion-2026} find tool overuse correlates with model uncertainty; \citet{when2tool-2026} show tool-call decisions are encoded in hidden states; \citet{tool-use-tax-2026} quantify a ``tool-use tax''; \citet{to-call-or-not-2026} propose a necessity-utility-affordability framework. Our decomposition provides environment-grounded evidence: in ScienceWorld, 78.9\% of routing labels trace to catastrophic-failure prevention.

\paragraph{Adaptive Computation and Routing.}
Adaptive routing traces back to learned halting policies \citep{graves2016act}. In the LLM era, CogRouter \cite{cogrouter-2026} adapts reasoning depth per step, ARES \cite{ares-2026} selects among reasoning effort levels, Mixture-of-Recursions \cite{mixture-of-recursions-2025} learns token-level recursion depths, and MTRouter \cite{mtrouter-2026} addresses multi-turn routing.
RouteLLM \citep{routellm-2024} learns routers from preference data to select between strong and weak models.
These optimize \emph{how} to route; we reveal \emph{why} routing helps: in interactive environments, the dominant signal is catastrophic-failure avoidance.

\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation in RL.}
Prior work generates counterfactual explanations for RL agents by perturbing individual actions within a fixed policy \citep{olson2021counterfactual, gajcin2023counterfactual, amitai2024explaining}.
Our approach differs in both purpose and granularity: we execute two complete episodes under different routing strategies, producing prospective paired comparisons that isolate routing signal \emph{before} any router is trained.

\paragraph{LLM Agent Environments and Reasoning.}
ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022} serves as our primary testbed; ALFWorld \cite{alfworld-2021} provides a complementary household environment; AgentBench \cite{agentbench-2024} offers systematic evaluation. \citet{zhou2024webarena} document common failure modes (premature termination, hallucinated actions) in web-based agents, consistent with our finding that state hallucination is the primary failure mechanism. ReAct \cite{react-2023} serves as our internal baseline; Reflexion \cite{reflexion-2023} extends this with verbal self-reflection; LATS \cite{lats-2024} adds tree search.

\paragraph{Safe RL.}
Death avoidance as an implicit routing signal connects to safe RL. Constrained MDPs \citep{altman1999constrained} formalize safety through state or cost constraints; our mechanism can be viewed as an implicit absorbing-state constraint. Risk-sensitive RL \citep{mihatsch2002risk} optimizes CVaR-type objectives; our framework diagnoses when such risk-sensitivity emerges rather than prescribing it. Classical methods \citep{garcia2015comprehensive,tamar2015cvar,achiam2017cpo,crpo-2021,moldovan2012safe} require explicit constraint specification; our routing achieves safety implicitly through computation mode selection. \citet{ruan2024failures} provide a taxonomy of LM agent failures identifying catastrophic actions as a distinct category, consistent with our finding. SafeRoute \citep{saferoute-2025} applies adaptive model selection for safety guardrails.

